{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-8b486c9f1734bbd57225","statement":"We make a few remarks.\n\n- Perhaps what we defined should be called quasi-separated (quasi-)placid stacks, but such generality is enough for our purpose. Note that a quasi-placid atlas is an epimorphism in $v$-topology. Therefore,\nby $v$-descent of $\\cshv$, there is a good theory of constructible sheaves on quasi-placid stacks, as we shall see in <<ref:sec-constructible.sheaves.on.placid.stacks>>. However, <<ref:ex:sheaves-example-descent>> shows that $v$-descent fails for $\\sshv$ (and for $\\shv$) in general so the category of all sheaves on a quasi-placid stack could be wild. We also note that the topological space associated to a quasi-placid stack could be quite wild. (See <<cite:Scholze.etale.cohomology.diamonds>> for some discussions in a different but related setup.)\nThese are the reasons we impose stronger condition in the definition of placid stacks. In particular, if $X$ is placid, then $|U_i|\\to |X|$ is a quasi-compact\\footnote{Quasi-compactness follows from our convention of representable morphisms. See <<ref:rem: qcqs representable>>.} open map and $|X|$ is a quasi-separated spectral topological space. In particular, a placid stack is quasi-compact if and only if the underlying topological space $|X|$ is quasi-compact.\n\n- Note that if the quasi-placid atlas $\\{U_i\\to X\\}_i$ is an effective epimorphism in \\'etale topology, then it is a placid atlas. In particular, combined with <<cite:bouthier2020perverse>>, we get that (perfect) $1$-placid stacks in the sense of <<cite:bouthier2020perverse>> are placid stacks in the terminology of this paper.\\footnote{But not conversely. E.g. the prestack $\\bB K$ from <<ref:ess.pro.p.torsor.hom.descent>> would not be $1$-placid in the sense of <<cite:bouthier2020perverse>>.} Moreover, one could develop an analogous theory of $\\infty$-stacks and $\\infty$-smooth morphisms as in loc. cit. The main results of this section generalize to that setting as well.","source_id":"arXiv:2504.07482","cluster":"representation_theory","statement_sha256":"8bd5ca43d6fa168487c246209597680fea1f32db74e9023a3d373f4e532c5d9e","kind":"remark","label":"Remark 804","ref_key":"rem-cond-on-placid-stacks","name":"","order":1128,"deps":[{"ref":"ess.pro.p.torsor.hom.descent","card_id":"ma-c-b93ffcf1ccfe413c43d3","label":"Proposition 799","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"ex:sheaves-example-descent","card_id":"ma-c-22363fbe9c52c8fdf30a","label":"Example 713","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"rem: qcqs representable","card_id":"ma-c-0ecda610863b37171a41","label":"Remark 773","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"sec-constructible.sheaves.on.placid.stacks","card_id":null,"label":null,"resolved":false,"target":"section"}],"cite_keys":["Scholze.etale.cohomology.diamonds","bouthier2020perverse"],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07482","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"1b700e8c4a2fc6f0c0f522039f223820ba6737a9a0ac91aea37c58ba7a3389c2","line":20757,"origin":"latex_env","retrieved_at":"2026-09-28T11:10:54+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v2","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_order_with_label_heuristic","detail":{"stripped_proof":false,"chars":1930,"math_hints":42}}},"paper":{"source_id":"arXiv:2504.07482","title":"Tame categorical local Langlands correspondence","abstract":"In one of our previous articles, we outlined the formulation of a version of the categorical arithmetic local Langlands conjecture. The aims of this article are threefold. First, we provide a detailed account of one component of this conjecture: the local Langlands category. Second, we aim to prove this conjecture in the tame case for quasi-split unramified reductive groups. Finally, we will explore the first applications of such categorical equivalence.","authors":["Xinwen Zhu"],"year":"2025","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07482","cluster":"representation_theory","macros":{